Make GiNaC::power::real_part()/imag_part() use their integer-binomial branch for an INEXACT
whole-number exponent too, instead of falling through to the polar form.

Both decide whether the (exact, rational) binomial expansion of Re/Im of (a+I*b)^N applies by asking
exponent.info(integer). An inexact 2.0 does not satisfy that even though the expansion is just as
valid for it, so they take the general branch

    |X|^c * exp(-d*atan2(b,a)) * cos(c*atan2(b,a) + d*log|X|)

which is mathematically right and useless to a caller that must not have the basis appear inside a
transcendental function. In pyoomph it is the dimensional analysis of a residual that then fails:
the basis is a mass density, atan2 hides its units from collect_base_units(), and code generation
aborts with "The added residual contribution is not dimensionless. It still carries the base units:
kilogram, meter". The exponent reached that state through a cache that compared the exact -2 to a
stored -2.0 - GiNaC hashes and compares numbers by value, and ex::compare() then unifies the two by
pointer, rewriting the power in place.

That cache is fixed in pyoomph, and keeping numbers exact is where the problem belongs. This is the
backstop for the whole class: an exponent that is an integer VALUE is treated as the integer it is,
by redoing the call with the exact number. Nothing else changes - every case that already took the
binomial branch had an exact exponent and is untouched.

This patch applies on top of ginac-binomial-real-imag-pow00.patch (it shares its context) and is
worth sending upstream together with it.

--- a/ginac/power.cpp
+++ b/ginac/power.cpp
@@ -673,8 +673,38 @@
 	return pow(x, n);
 }
 
+// An exponent that is an inexact numeric happening to be a whole number - 2.0 rather than 2 - as
+// the exact integer it is, or the expression unchanged if it is not one. real_part() and
+// imag_part() below decide whether they may use the (exact, real) integer-binomial expansion by
+// asking exponent.info(integer), which an inexact 2.0 does not satisfy even though the expansion is
+// just as valid for it. They then fall through to the general polar form
+// |X|^c*exp(-d*atan2(b,a))*cos(c*atan2(b,a)+d*log|X|), which is correct but useless to any caller
+// that has to keep the result free of transcendental functions of the basis - in pyoomph, the
+// dimensional analysis of a residual, which cannot separate the units of a basis that has landed
+// inside an atan2. Numbers should of course be kept exact in the first place; this is the backstop.
+static ex exact_whole_number_exponent(const ex & e)
+{
+	if (!is_exactly_a<numeric>(e))
+		return e;
+	const numeric & n = ex_to<numeric>(e);
+	if (n.is_crational() || !n.is_real())
+		return e;
+	const double d = n.to_double();
+	if (d > 1e15 || d < -1e15)
+		return e;
+	const long l = (long)d;
+	if ((double)l != d)
+		return e;
+	return numeric(l);
+}
+
 ex power::real_part() const
 {
+	{
+		const ex xexponent = exact_whole_number_exponent(exponent);
+		if (!are_ex_trivially_equal(xexponent, exponent))
+			return pow(basis, xexponent).real_part();
+	}
 	// basis == a+I*b, exponent == c+I*d
 	const ex a = basis.real_part();
 	const ex c = exponent.real_part();
@@ -711,6 +741,11 @@
 
 ex power::imag_part() const
 {
+	{
+		const ex xexponent = exact_whole_number_exponent(exponent);
+		if (!are_ex_trivially_equal(xexponent, exponent))
+			return pow(basis, xexponent).imag_part();
+	}
 	// basis == a+I*b, exponent == c+I*d
 	const ex a = basis.real_part();
 	const ex c = exponent.real_part();
